The time() function is a standard library function defined in the <time.h> header file in C and <ctime> in C++. It returns the current calendar time as the number of seconds elapsed since 00:00:00 UTC, January 1, 1970 (Unix Epoch).Returns the current time as a Unix timestamp in seconds.Can also store the returned time in a user-provided time_t object if a valid pointer is passed.
            C

    #include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

int main ()
{
    time_t seconds;

    seconds = time(NULL);
    printf("Seconds since January 1, 1970 = %ld\n", seconds);

    return(0);
}

OutputSeconds since January 1, 1970 = 1538123990Syntax  time_t time( time_t *second )Parameter: This function accepts single parameter second. This parameter is used to set the time_t object which store the time.Return Value: This function returns current calendar time as a object of type time_t.Advantages The time() function provides a simple and portable way to obtain the current system time for various time-related operations.Returns the current calendar time as a Unix timestamp in seconds.Can directly store the current time in a time_t object through the function parameter.Widely supported across C and C++ compilers, making it portable.Limitations Although time() is useful for obtaining the current time, it has some limitations.Returns time only in seconds, so it cannot measure milliseconds or microseconds.Depends on the system clock, so incorrect system time affects the returned value.Suitable for general timekeeping but not for high-precision performance measurements.
